    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the specific intermediates formed after the Schiff base stage? I'm looking for the more complex rearrangement products, like the Amadori or Heyns rearrangements, and how those eventually lead to the melanoidins that give the food its color and flavor profile.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Following the formation of the Schiff base, an important intramolecular proton transfer leads to the Amadori rearrangement when modifying aldoses, or the Heyns rearrangement when modifying ketoses. These stable, intermediate compounds then undergo a cascade of complex degradation and dehydration reactions. This series of non-enzymatic reactions fragments and polymerizes these early intermediates, ultimately forming the high-molecular-weight, brown-pigmented compounds known as melanoidins. These melanoidins are responsible for the desirable color, aroma, and flavor characteristics in cooked and toasted foods.",
      "source": "target",
